<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
<question type="stack">
<name>
<text>Would like author randomised stack probability</text>
</name>
<questiontext format="html">
<text><![CDATA[<p>A bag contains 16 counters: {@nr@} red, {@nb@} blue and {@ng@} green. Two counters are taken at random from the bag, one after the other, without replacement. The probability tree is shown below. The probabilities labelled \(a\), \(b\) and \(c\) are missing.</p>
[[jsxgraph width="560px" height="420px"]]
var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {boundingbox:[-0.5,10.5,11,-0.5], axis:false, showCopyright:false, showNavigation:false});
var L1 = {#l1#};
var L2 = {#l2#};
var cols = ["Red","Blue","Green"];
var y1 = [8.5,5,1.5];
var dy = [1.1,0,-1.1];
var root = board.create('point',[0.5,5],{name:'',size:2,fixed:true,withLabel:false});
for (var i=0;i<3;i++) {
  var p = board.create('point',[4.5,y1[i]],{name:'',size:2,fixed:true,withLabel:false});
  board.create('segment',[root,p],{fixed:true,highlight:false});
  board.create('text',[2.0,(5+y1[i])/2+0.3,L1[i]],{fixed:true});
  board.create('text',[4.7,y1[i]+0.3,cols[i]],{fixed:true});
  for (var j=0;j<3;j++) {
    var yy = y1[i]+dy[j];
    var q = board.create('point',[8.5,yy],{name:'',size:2,fixed:true,withLabel:false});
    board.create('segment',[p,q],{fixed:true,highlight:false});
    board.create('text',[6.0,(y1[i]+yy)/2+0.15,L2[3*i+j]],{fixed:true});
    board.create('text',[8.7,yy-0.1,cols[j]],{fixed:true});
  }
}
[[/jsxgraph]]
<p>Enter all answers as fractions (they need not be simplified).</p>
<p>(i) Find \(a\) = P(blue on 1st draw): [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt_ans1]]</p>
<p>\(b\) = P(blue on 2nd | blue on 1st): [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt_ans2]]</p>
<p>\(c\) = P(red on 2nd | blue on 1st): [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt_ans3]]</p>
<p>(ii) Find the probability that both counters are blue: [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt_ans4]]</p>
<p>(iii) Find the probability that both counters are the same colour: [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt_ans5]]</p>]]></text>
</questiontext>
<questionvariables>
<text>[nr,nb,ng]: random_permutation(rand([[2,3,11],[2,4,10],[2,5,9],[2,6,8],[3,4,9],[3,5,8],[3,6,7],[4,5,7]]));
l1: [sconcat(nr,"/16"),"a",sconcat(ng,"/16")];
l2: [sconcat(nr-1,"/15"),sconcat(nb,"/15"),sconcat(ng,"/15"),"c","b",sconcat(ng,"/15"),sconcat(nr,"/15"),sconcat(nb,"/15"),sconcat(ng-1,"/15")];
ta1: nb/16;
ta2: (nb-1)/15;
ta3: nr/15;
ta4: nb*(nb-1)/240;
rrt: nr*(nr-1)/240;
ggt: ng*(ng-1)/240;
ta5: (nr*(nr-1)+nb*(nb-1)+ng*(ng-1))/240;</text>
</questionvariables>
<generalfeedback format="html">
<text><![CDATA[<p>At the start there are 16 counters, {@nb@} of them blue, so \(a = {@ta1@}\) (as \({@nb@}/16\)).</p>
<p>After a blue counter is taken (and not replaced), 15 counters remain: {@nb-1@} blue, {@nr@} red and {@ng@} green. So \(b = {@nb-1@}/15\) and \(c = {@nr@}/15\). The three branches after blue add up to 1.</p>
<p>(ii) Multiply along the blue–blue branches: \(P(BB) = {@nb@}/16 \times {@nb-1@}/15 = {@ta4@}\).</p>
<p>(iii) Find each same-colour path: \(P(RR)={@nr@}/16\times{@nr-1@}/15\), \(P(BB)={@nb@}/16\times{@nb-1@}/15\), \(P(GG)={@ng@}/16\times{@ng-1@}/15\). Adding them gives \({@ta5@}\).</p>]]></text>
</generalfeedback>
<specificfeedback format="html">
  <text></text>
</specificfeedback>
<questionnote format="moodle_auto_format">
<text>Counts red {@nr@}, blue {@nb@}, green {@ng@}</text>
</questionnote>
